= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

<Nielsen's pure-state conversion theorem> concerns exact deterministic transformations of bipartite <pure states> by <local operations and classical communication>. Let $\lambda(\psi)$ be the decreasing vector of squared <Schmidt coefficients>, equivalently the <eigenvalues> of either party's <reduced density matrix>, padded with zeros when necessary. Then
$$
\boxed{|\psi\rangle\longrightarrow|\chi\rangle\text{ by deterministic LOCC}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\lambda(\psi)\prec\lambda(\chi).}
$$
The <majorization> relation means $\sum_{j=1}^k\lambda_j(\psi)\le\sum_{j=1}^k\lambda_j(\chi)$ for every $k$, with equality for the total sum. The direction allows a maximally entangled state to become a <product state>, not the reverse. <Local-unitary invariance of reduced-state spectra> alone would require equality of all the weights; measurements and communicated outcomes permit this larger class of conversions. A comparison of <entanglement entropies> alone is not a sufficient test in higher dimension.<Conditions for a class of entanglement transformations by Michael A. Nielsen>[Necessary and sufficient majorization criterion for deterministic bipartite pure-state conversion]{ref}

For the supplied states, the weight vectors and cumulative sums are
$$
\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
\lambda(\psi_1)&0.4&0.4&0.1&0.1\\
\lambda(\psi_2)&0.5&0.25&0.25&0
\end{array}\qquad
\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
\text{input sums}&0.4&0.8&0.9&1\\
\text{output sums}&0.5&0.75&1&1
\end{array}
$$
At $k=2$, $0.8>0.75$, violating the required inequality. **The desired conversion is impossible with certainty using LOCC alone.** The reverse conversion also fails, already at $k=1$, so these states are incomparable under this deterministic ordering.

Taking a tensor product with the catalyst multiplies every original weight by each catalyst weight. Regrouping the two local systems on Alice's side and the two on Bob's side leaves a <Schmidt decomposition> across the Alice–Bob partition. After sorting, the resulting weight vectors are
$$
\begin{aligned}
x&=(0.24,0.24,0.16,0.16,0.06,0.06,0.04,0.04),\\
y&=(0.30,0.20,0.15,0.15,0.10,0.10,0,0).
\end{aligned}
$$
The actual nonnegative Schmidt amplitudes are $\sqrt{x_j}$ and $\sqrt{y_j}$; the displayed numbers are their squares. The cumulative sums are
$$
\begin{aligned}
X&=(0.24,0.48,0.64,0.80,0.86,0.92,0.96,1),\\
Y&=(0.30,0.50,0.65,0.80,0.90,1,1,1).
\end{aligned}
$$
Every component obeys $X_k\le Y_k$, so $x\prec y$. Hence
$$
\boxed{|\psi_1\rangle\otimes|\phi\rangle
\xrightarrow{\rm LOCC}|\psi_2\rangle\otimes|\phi\rangle.}
$$
The final catalyst is exactly its initial state and is uncorrelated with the desired output. It can therefore be returned and reused. This is <entanglement catalysis>; it is stronger than merely retaining the same catalyst marginal while leaving correlations with the output.<Entanglement-assisted local manipulation of pure quantum states by Daniel Jonathan and Martin B. Plenio>[Two-qubit catalyst example and unchanged product-state return]{ref}

For the maximally entangled two-level auxiliary state, the sorted input weights are $(0.2,0.2,0.2,0.2,0.05,0.05,0.05,0.05)$, while the output weights are $(0.25,0.25,0.125,0.125,0.125,0.125,0,0)$. Their first four sums are $0.8$ and $0.75$. **This maximally entangled auxiliary state cannot catalyze the conversion while being returned unchanged.**

Nevertheless, $(0.5,0.5)\prec(0.6,0.4)$, so it can first be converted deterministically into the nonmaximal catalyst. The desired conversion can then proceed, after which Alice and Bob can return **$|\phi\rangle$, rather than the borrowed maximally entangled state**. Turning that returned pair alone back into a maximally entangled pair would increase its <entanglement entropy> and is forbidden by LOCC. No joint restoration procedure can both keep the exact desired $|\psi_2\rangle$ and return the original maximally entangled auxiliary state deterministically: its composition with the preceding steps would contradict the failed four-weight inequality above.

\Image[paper-1-catalytic-majorization.png]
{title=The catalyst repairs every failed majorization inequality, while a maximally entangled pair does not}

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The input size of an <integer factorization> problem is its number of bits $n\simeq\log_2N$. Trial division up to $\sqrt N$ is exponential in $n$; advanced classical algorithms do substantially better, but no general polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm is known. The heuristic general-purpose <general number field sieve> has subexponential rather than polynomial scaling, roughly $\exp(O(n^{1/3}(\log n)^{2/3}))$. This describes known algorithmic performance, not a proof that efficient classical factoring is impossible or that every large integer is difficult.

Choose $a$ with $1<a<N$. A nontrivial <greatest common divisor> $\gcd(a,N)$ already supplies a factor; otherwise find the <multiplicative order> $r$ of $a$ modulo $N$. If $r$ is even and $a^{r/2}\not\equiv-1\pmod N$, then
$$
(a^{r/2}-1)(a^{r/2}+1)\equiv0\pmod N.
$$
Minimality of the order also excludes $a^{r/2}\equiv1\pmod N$, so <factor extraction from an even modular order> gives nontrivial factors through $\gcd(a^{r/2}\pm1,N)$. Unhelpful choices of $a$ or odd orders require another trial. For $N=15$, $a=2$ has successive residues $1,2,4,8$ and order four, giving
$$
\boxed{\gcd(2^2-1,15)=3,\qquad\gcd(2^2+1,15)=5.}
$$
Computing the powers is easy by <repeated squaring>; discovering a large order is the step accelerated by <Shor's algorithm>.<Polynomial-Time Algorithms for Prime Factorization and Discrete Logarithms on a Quantum Computer by Peter W. Shor>[Quantum reduction to order finding and classical factor extraction]{ref}

Put $Q=2^L$ and suppose $Q\ge r$. For each residue class $s=0,\ldots,r-1$, let
$$
n_s=\left\lfloor\frac{Q-1-s}{r}\right\rfloor+1,\qquad
|\phi_s\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt{n_s}}\sum_{j=0}^{n_s-1}|s+jr\rangle.
$$
These are normalized uniform superpositions of the arguments with the same modular-exponentiation value. Their amplitudes repeat at separation $r$ within the finite register window. Exact cyclic periodicity requires $r\mid Q$. The correctly normalized entangled register state is
$$
\boxed{\frac1{\sqrt Q}\sum_{x=0}^{Q-1}|x\rangle|a^x\bmod N\rangle
=\sum_{s=0}^{r-1}\sqrt{\frac{n_s}{Q}}\,|\phi_s\rangle|a^s\bmod N\rangle.}
$$
The equal prefactor $1/\sqrt r$ in the displayed algorithm is exact when $r\mid Q$, for then every $n_s=Q/r$. It is not generally exact for an arbitrary unknown order. A physical implementation uses <quantum modular exponentiation> as a reversible map; its specified action on second-register input zero is extended to a <unitary operator> on the whole register space.

For the illustrative $N=15$, $a=2$ and $Q=16$, the four first-register states are
$$
|\phi_s\rangle=\frac12\bigl(|s\rangle+|s+4\rangle+|s+8\rangle+|s+12\rangle\bigr),\qquad s=0,1,2,3.
$$
Applying the positive-sign <quantum Fourier transform> to the general state gives
$$
\operatorname{QFT}|\phi_s\rangle
=\frac1{\sqrt{Qn_s}}\sum_{k=0}^{Q-1}e^{2\pi iks/Q}
\left[\sum_{j=0}^{n_s-1}e^{2\pi ikrj/Q}\right]|k\rangle.
$$
The bracket is a <finite geometric Fourier amplitude>. If $r\mid Q$, it vanishes unless $k=\ell Q/r$ for $\ell=0,\ldots,r-1$, when it equals $Q/r$. Hence
$$
\operatorname{QFT}|\phi_s\rangle
=\frac1{\sqrt r}\sum_{\ell=0}^{r-1}e^{2\pi i\ell s/r}|\ell Q/r\rangle.
$$
The second-register states are orthogonal, so their different phases do not change the marginal first-register probabilities. Measurement gives
$$
\boxed{k=\ell Q/r\quad\text{with probability }1/r.}
$$
In the $Q=16$ example the possible outcomes are $0,4,8,12$. A nonzero observed ratio $k/Q$ estimates $\ell/r$, not a prime factor directly. Reducing it gives denominator $r/\gcd(\ell,r)$; repeating avoids uninformative outcomes or recovers missing order factors. For example, $k=4$ or $12$ gives denominator four, whereas $k=8$ alone gives only two.

When $r\nmid Q$, the Fourier probabilities have narrow peaks near $k/Q=\ell/r$ instead of support only on an exact reciprocal grid. The general marginal distribution is
$$
\Pr(k)=\frac1{Q^2}\sum_{s=0}^{r-1}
\left|\sum_{j=0}^{n_s-1}e^{2\pi ikrj/Q}\right|^2.
$$
Choosing $Q\ge N^2$ supplies the usual precision for <continued-fraction recovery in quantum order finding>: a nearest grid sample differs from the target rational by at most $1/(2Q)<1/(2r^2)$. Candidate denominators and extracted divisors are checked classically, and repetitions provide useful samples. The small $Q=16$ example works exactly because its order divides the register size; it is not a general precision guarantee for factoring every four-bit integer.

\Image[paper-1-order-finding-fourier.png]
{title=Exact reciprocal-period sampling for order four and broadened peaks when the order does not divide the register}

A sufficiently large reliable quantum computer would undermine <RSA> by efficient factorization. The related discrete-logarithm algorithm also threatens <Diffie-Hellman key exchange> and elliptic-curve public-key schemes. This does not imply that every communication scheme or symmetric cipher is broken by the same algorithm; <post-quantum cryptography> studies alternatives based on other problems. The mathematical speedup assumes a scalable quantum computation with enough precision and error control, not merely a small demonstration of factoring fifteen.

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Consistent histories> assigns probabilities to suitably compatible sequences of quantum alternatives without making an external measuring apparatus fundamental. At successive times choose complete orthogonal sets of <projection operators>. In the <Heisenberg picture>, a history has class operator $C_\alpha=P_{\alpha_n}(t_n)\cdots P_{\alpha_1}(t_1)$. For an initial <density operator> $\rho$, its <decoherence functional> is
$$
D(\alpha,\beta)=\operatorname{tr}(C_\alpha\rho C_\beta^\dagger).
$$
A common sufficient consistency condition is $D(\alpha,\beta)=0$ for $\alpha\ne\beta$. Then $p(\alpha)=D(\alpha,\alpha)$ is nonnegative and coarse-grained alternatives have additive probabilities: the <interference> cross-terms vanish. The weaker requirement on real cross-terms can suffice for individual <probability> sum rules. Predictions must stay within one compatible framework; mutually incompatible history families cannot simply be combined into one classical account.

The <many-worlds interpretation> instead treats unitary quantum evolution as universally valid and interprets the correlated outcome branches as components of one global state. A measurement interaction gives
$$
(c_0|0\rangle+c_1|1\rangle)|A_{\rm ready}\rangle|E_{\rm ready}\rangle
\longrightarrow c_0|0\rangle|A_0\rangle|E_0\rangle+c_1|1\rangle|A_1\rangle|E_1\rangle.
$$
When the environment records become nearly orthogonal, <quantum decoherence> suppresses observable <interference> between stable apparatus records. Each <relative state> contains an observer correlated with a definite record; no additional physical collapse is postulated. Branch structure is an emergent approximation, rather than a unique literal splitting at every arbitrary choice of basis.

Both approaches use the ordinary unitary formalism and decoherence, but their interpretations of histories and branches differ. Decoherence alone does not prove that one unique branch is selected, nor does the unitary equation by itself settle every assumption needed to justify <Born rule> probabilities. Neither interpretation predicts ordinary <interference> experiments incorrectly merely by interpreting the same state differently.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In <quantum teleportation>, Alice transfers an arbitrary unknown <qubit> $|\chi\rangle=\alpha|0\rangle+\beta|1\rangle$ using one shared <Bell pair> and two bits of <classical communication>. Define $|\beta_{mn}\rangle=(I\otimes X^nZ^m)|\Phi^+\rangle$, with $m,n\in\{0,1\}$. Regrouping the initial three-qubit state into this <Bell basis> gives
$$
|\chi\rangle_1|\Phi^+\rangle_{23}
=\frac12\sum_{m,n}|\beta_{mn}\rangle_{12}X^nZ^m|\chi\rangle_3.
$$
Alice's <Bell-basis measurement> selects each label with <probability> $1/4$. She sends $(m,n)$ to Bob, who applies the inverse $Z^mX^n$, restoring $|\chi\rangle$. The correction depends on the measurement label, not on the unknown amplitudes. Before the message, Bob's average state is $I/2$, independent of the input; <quantum no-signalling> therefore prevents superluminal communication. The shared pair is consumed and Alice does not retain an independent copy, in agreement with the <no-cloning theorem>.

The <Bouwmeester quantum teleportation experiment> used <photon> <polarization>, a down-conversion entangled pair, a beam-splitter Bell-state analyzer and coincidence-conditioned output <polarization> measurements. Temporal indistinguishability of the two analyzer <photons> was essential. Its successful selected Bell outcome demonstrated the state transfer, rather than a deterministic implementation of all four outcomes.<Experimental quantum teleportation by Dik Bouwmeester and others>[1997, experimental realization and Figures 1–5]{ref}

The general experimental tasks are to distribute entanglement, prepare and synchronize the input, perform a sufficiently discriminating joint measurement, communicate the label and apply or account for the correction. A demonstration must test nonorthogonal input states, not merely transfer a known classical basis label. Finite efficiency, accidental coincidences, distinguishability and phase drift reduce success <probability> or <teleportation fidelity>. Postselected verification, a heralded usable output and unconditional deterministic teleportation are distinct experimental claims.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<Schrödinger's cat> exposes the <quantum measurement problem> by correlating a microscopic alternative with macroscopically distinct records. Under unitary evolution a schematic state becomes
$$
\alpha|\text{undecayed}\rangle|\text{alive}\rangle
+\beta|\text{decayed}\rangle|\text{dead}\rangle.
$$
The difficulty is explaining why a definite macroscopic outcome is observed if the isolated total state is always governed by the linear <Schrödinger equation>. It is not a claim that one classically contradictory record has literally been observed. Environmental interactions rapidly entangle the records with different environment states, making their reduced <density matrix> approximately diagonal. That explains the practical loss of <interference> but does not, by itself, turn a global pure superposition into a selected single branch.

<Wigner's friend> places an observer's physical memory inside the quantum description. A friend measuring $|+\rangle=(|0\rangle+|1\rangle)/\sqrt2$ can become correlated as
$$
|+\rangle|F_{\rm ready}\rangle
\longrightarrow |\Psi\rangle=\frac{|0\rangle|F_0\rangle+|1\rangle|F_1\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
$$
Conditioned on one friend record, the corresponding <relative state> contains a definite outcome. An external observer applying unitary theory to the perfectly isolated entire laboratory instead assigns the entangled vector $|\Psi\rangle$. In principle, a coherent joint measurement could distinguish it from the collapsed mixture
$$
\rho_{\rm mix}=\frac12\bigl(|0F_0\rangle\langle0F_0|+|1F_1\rangle\langle1F_1|\bigr):
\qquad \langle\Psi|\rho_{\rm mix}|\Psi\rangle=\frac12,
$$
whereas projecting the pure state onto $|\Psi\rangle$ succeeds with <probability> one. Preserving that laboratory coherence, including all records and environment correlations, is an extreme idealization for macroscopic observers.

The paradox concerns how state assignment, definite records and universal unitary dynamics fit together. It does not show that an observer can send messages faster than light or that consciousness must be given a special dynamical role. The <many-worlds interpretation> retains the global unitary state with branch-relative records; interpretations with a physical collapse must specify when and how unitary evolution changes. Stating the chosen assumptions prevents the friend's conditional state and the external observer's unconditioned state from being treated as the same mathematical object.
